{
  "slug": "financial-anomaly-detection",
  "title": "Detect and Explain Anomalies in Financial Transaction Logs",
  "description": "At a payments company, the fraud team reviews transaction logs daily. Your pipeline receives a batch of transactions and must:\n\n1. Identify which transactions are **anomalous** (flag: true/false)\n2. For each anomaly, provide: anomaly_type (one of: velocity, amount, geo_impossible, pattern_break, round_trip, structuring), confidence (0.0–1.0), explanation (human-readable, 1-2 sentences)\n3. Identify **transaction chains** — groups of related transactions that together form a suspicious pattern even if individually normal\n\nOutput a JSON object with: flagged_transactions (array of objects with transaction_id, anomaly_type, confidence, explanation) and suspicious_chains (array of objects with chain_id, transaction_ids, pattern, explanation).\n\nThe challenge: most transactions are normal. The anomalies are subtle — they rely on cross-referencing timestamps, amounts, counterparties, and geolocation. A single $500 ATM withdrawal is normal; three $500 withdrawals in different cities within 2 hours is impossible. A $9,900 transfer is normal; five $9,900 transfers in a week (just under the $10K reporting threshold) is structuring.",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "category": "analysis",
  "tags": [
    "fintech",
    "fraud-detection",
    "anomaly-detection",
    "pattern-recognition",
    "compliance"
  ],
  "company_context": "Companies like Stripe Radar, PayPal Risk, and Featurespace build ML-powered fraud detection. But edge cases and new fraud patterns still need human-in-the-loop analysis. Engineers at Wise (TransferWise) report that AI-generated explanations for fraud flags reduced false positive review time by 50% — the explanation matters as much as the detection.",
  "input_format": "json",
  "output_format": "json",
  "constraints": {
    "max_llm_calls": 10,
    "allowed_models": [
      "gpt-4o",
      "gpt-4o-mini"
    ],
    "time_limit_seconds": 120
  },
